{"name":"Wax Sculpture","hex":"#e2d5bd","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"wax","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Watam."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any oily, water-resistant, solid or semisolid substance; normally long-chain hydrocarbons, alcohols or esters.","Beeswax (a wax secreted by bees)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/wax"},{"term":"sculpture","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A three-dimensional work of art created by shaping malleable objects and letting them harden or by chipping away pieces from a rock (sculpting).","More generally (since modernism), a three-dimensional work of art of any material or of mixed materials, whether sculpted, assembled, found, constructed or created using multiple techniques."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To fashion something into a three-dimensional figure.","To represent something in sculpture."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sculpture"}],"entries":[{"title":"Wax sculpture","blocks":[{"text":"A wax sculpture is a depiction made using a waxy substance. Often these are effigies, usually of a notable individual, but there are also death masks and scenes with many figures, mostly in relief."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Anna_von_Tirol.jpg/330px-Anna_von_Tirol.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wax_sculpture"},{"title":"Wax","blocks":[{"text":"Waxes are a diverse class of organic compounds that are lipophilic solids that are malleable near ambient temperatures. They include higher alkanes and lipids, typically with melting points above about 40 °C (104 °F), melting to give low viscosity liquids. Waxes are insoluble in water but soluble in nonpolar organic solvents such as hexane, benzene and chloroform. Natural waxes of various types are produced by plants and animals and occur in petroleum."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/Beeswax_foundation.jpg/330px-Beeswax_foundation.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wax"},{"title":"Sculpture","blocks":[{"text":"Sculpture is the branch of the visual arts that operates in three dimensions. Sculpture is the three-dimensional artwork which is physically presented in the dimensions of height, width and depth. It is one of the plastic arts. Durable sculptural processes originally used carving and modelling, in stone, metal, ceramics, wood and other materials but, since Modernism, there has been almost complete freedom of materials and process. A wide variety of materials may be worked by removal such as carving, assembled by welding or modelling, or moulded or cast."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Dying_gaul.jpg/330px-Dying_gaul.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sculpture"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/2f/Elizabeth_of_york_-_funeral_effigy.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/2f/Elizabeth_of_york_-_funeral_effigy.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"The funeral effigy (without clothes) of Elizabeth of York, mother of King Henry VIII, 1503, Westminster Abbey","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AElizabeth_of_york_-_funeral_effigy.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/The_Beatles_wax_dummies.jpg/500px-The_Beatles_wax_dummies.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/The_Beatles_wax_dummies.jpg/500px-The_Beatles_wax_dummies.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaig